WEBVTT

00:21:28.863 --> 00:21:30.883
In the first few minutes of the interview,

00:21:30.907 --> 00:21:32.626
it was clear he was a bad fit.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:34.416
He hardly made any eye contact.

00:21:35.162 --> 00:21:36.375
When I told my boss

00:21:36.399 --> 00:21:38.456
I wasn't going to hire him
because of that,

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:42.477
she said, "You know
this is a phone sales job, right?"

00:21:43.505 --> 00:21:44.691
It left me wondering

00:21:44.715 --> 00:21:47.532
if I should stop making
hiring decisions altogether.

00:21:48.008 --> 00:21:50.241
Maybe I should just let the robots do it.

00:21:50.805 --> 00:21:53.728
Cade Massey: If I had a horse race
between algorithms doing it

00:21:53.752 --> 00:21:55.851
and a person doing it,

00:21:55.875 --> 00:21:57.790
I mean, in most domains,

00:21:57.814 --> 00:22:00.045
I'm going to end up
favoring the algorithm.